L1: On the maximum force conjecture in curved spacetimes of stable self-gravitating matter configurations
L2: Shahar Hod
L3: The Ruppin Academic Center, Emeq Hefer 40250, Israel
L4: and
L5: The Hadassah Institute, Jerusalem 91010, Israel
L6: (Dated: January 6, 2025)
L7: Gibbons and Schiller have raised the physically interesting conjecture that forces in general relativity are bounded from above by the mathematically compact relation \mathcal{F} \leq c^4/4G. In the present compact paper we explicitly prove, using the non-linearly coupled Einstein-matter field equations, that the force function \mathcal{F} \equiv 4\pi r^2 p(r) in stable self-gravitating horizonless matter configurations is characterized by the upper bound \mathcal{F} \leq c^4/G [here p(r) is the radial pressure inside the self-gravitating matter configuration].
L8: I. INTRODUCTION
L9: The maximum force conjecture in general relativity was raised by Gibbons [1] and Schiller [2, 3] more than two decades ago. This intriguing conjecture asserts that, within the framework of general relativity, forces are bounded from above by a classical (no-\hbar) relation of the form
L10: \mathcal{F} \leq \eta \cdot \frac{c^4}{G}, \quad (1)
L11: where \eta is a dimensionless factor. In particular, the strong version of the maximum force conjecture asserts that \eta = 1/4 in the inequality (1) [1, 2], whereas \eta = O(1) in the weak version of the conjecture [4]. Interestingly, it was claimed [5] that the maximum force relation (1) can be used to derive the Einstein-matter field equations of general relativity.
L12: In the present compact paper we raise the following physically important question: Can a maximum force relation of the form (1) be derived in a mathematically rigorous way for spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations in curved spacetimes?
L13: Interestingly, below we shall explicitly prove that the non-linearly coupled Einstein-matter field equations, supplemented by the physically motivated requirement of dynamical stability, yield an upper bound on the radially-dependent dimensionless force function [6]
L14: \mathcal{F} \equiv 4\pi r^2 \cdot p(r) \quad (2)
L15: in self-gravitating matter configurations.
L16: In particular, we shall explicitly show below that the existence of an upper bound on the magnitude of the force function (2) in curved spacetimes of self-gravitating field configurations is closely related to the mathematically elegant theorem presented in [7] (see also [8, 9]), according to which the innermost light ring (the innermost closed null circular geodesic) of an horizonless curved spacetime, if it exists, is stable [10].
L17: As nicely discussed in [11, 12] (see also [13]), the presence of stable light rings in spatially regular spacetimes of self-gravitating matter configurations implies that the corresponding curved spacetimes develop non-linear instabilities in the presence of massless fields [14]. One therefore concludes that a necessary condition for self-gravitating matter configurations to be dynamically stable is that the corresponding curved spacetimes do not possess null circular geodesics (closed light rings).
L18: Motivated by the physically intriguing observation made in [7, 11, 12] regarding the (in)stability properties of spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations in classical general relativity, in the present paper we shall use the non-linearly coupled Einstein-matter field equations in order to derive an explicit upper bound on the dimensionless force function (2) in stable curved spacetimes [15–17].
L19: II. DESCRIPTION OF THE SYSTEM
L20: We consider spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations whose curved spacetimes are described by the spherically symmetric line element [13, 18–21]
L21: ds^2 = -e^{-2\delta} \mu dt^2 + \mu^{-1} dr^2 + r^2 (d\theta^2 + \sin^2 \theta d\phi^2), \quad (3)
L22: where \mu = \mu(r) and \delta = \delta(r).
L23: The non-linearly coupled Einstein-matter field equations G_\nu^\mu = 8\pi T_\nu^\mu of the spherically symmetric spacetime (3) can be expressed in the form [13, 18, 22, 23]
L24: \mu' = -8\pi r \rho + \frac{1 - \mu}{r} \quad (4)
L25: and
L26: \delta' = -\frac{4\pi r(\rho + p)}{\mu} , \quad (5)
L27: where \rho \equiv -T_t^t and p \equiv T_r^r are respectively the energy density and the radial pressure that characterize the matter fields [24].
L28: The metric functions of spatially regular asymptotically flat spacetimes are characterized by the boundary relations [13, 22]:
L29: \mu(r \rightarrow 0) \rightarrow 1 \quad ; \quad \delta(r \rightarrow 0) &lt; \infty \quad (6)
L30: and
L31: \mu(r \rightarrow \infty) \rightarrow 1 \quad ; \quad \delta(r \rightarrow \infty) \rightarrow 0 . \quad (7)
L32: In addition, horizonless matter configurations, which are the focus of the present study, are characterized by the inequality
L33: \mu(r) &gt; 0 \quad \text{for} \quad r \in [0, \infty] . \quad (8)
L34: Taking cognizance of the Einstein equation (4) one finds that the dimensionless metric function \mu(r) can be expressed in the compact mathematical form [13, 22]
L35: \mu(r) = 1 - \frac{2m(r)}{r} , \quad (9)
L36: where
L37: m(r) = \int_0^r 4\pi x^2 \rho(x) dx \quad (10)
L38: is the radially-dependent gravitational mass contained within a sphere of radius r [13, 22].
L39: We shall assume that the spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations are characterized by the dominant energy condition,
L40: 0 \leq |p| \leq \rho , \quad (11)
L41: which implies the relation [see Eqs. (9) and (10)]
L42: \mu(r) \leq 1 \quad \text{for} \quad r \in [0, \infty] \quad (12)
L43: for the dimensionless metric function.
L44: For later purposes we note that Eq. (10) implies that asymptotically flat spacetimes (with finite ADM masses) are characterized by the asymptotic radial behavior
L45: r^3 \cdot \rho(r) \rightarrow 0 \quad \text{for} \quad r \rightarrow \infty , \quad (13)
L46: which also implies the asymptotic relation [see Eq. (11)]
L47: r^3 \cdot p(r) \rightarrow 0 \quad \text{for} \quad r \rightarrow \infty \quad (14)
L48: for the pressure function in the curved spacetime (3).
L49: III. MAXIMUM FORCE RELATION IN CURVED SPACETIMES OF STABLE SPATIALLY REGULAR SELF-GRAVITATING MATTER CONFIGURATIONS
L50: In the present section we shall explicitly prove that one can use the instability properties of horizonless spacetimes that possess null circular geodesics (closed light rings) [7–9, 11–13] in order to derive an upper bound on the dimensionless force function (2) that characterizes stable spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations.
L51: The radial functional relation that determines the radii of null circular geodesics in the curved spacetime (3) was derived in [18, 25]. Before we present our proof for the existence of an explicit upper bound on the dimensionless force function (2) in curved spacetimes of stable self-gravitating matter configurations, we shall first provide here a brief sketch of the analytical derivation of the characteristic equation [see Eq. (17) below] that determines the locations of closed light rings in spherically symmetric curved spacetimes.
L52: As explicitly shown in [18, 25], null circular geodesics are characterized by the functional relations [26, 27]
L53: V_r = E^2 \quad \text{and} \quad V_r' = 0, \quad (15)
L54: where the effective radial potential V_r is determined by the expression [18, 25]
L55: E^2 - V_r \equiv \dot{r}^2 = \mu \left( \frac{E^2}{e^{-2\delta}\mu} - \frac{L^2}{r^2} \right). \quad (16)
L56: Here the physical parameters \{E, L\} are respectively the energy and the angular momentum that characterize the geodesic motion in the curved spacetime. These conserved physical quantities reflect the fact that the spherically symmetric static spacetime (3) has no dependence on the time and angular coordinates \{t, \phi\} [18, 25].
L57: Substituting (16) into the functional relations (15) and taking cognizance of the Einstein equations (4) and (5), one finds that the radial relation
L58: \mathcal{R}(r = r_\gamma) = 0 \quad (17)
L59: with
L60: \mathcal{R}(r) \equiv 3\mu(r) - 1 - 8\pi r^2 p(r) \quad (18)
L61: determines the radii of null circular geodesics (closed light rings) in the spherically symmetric curved spacetime (3).
L62: In addition, from Eqs. (6), (7), (14), and (18) one learns that the dimensionless radial function \mathcal{R}(r) is characterized by the boundary relations
L63: \mathcal{R}(r = 0) = 2 \quad (19)
L64: and
L65: \mathcal{R}(r \rightarrow \infty) \rightarrow 2. \quad (20)
L66: Taking cognizance of Eqs. (17), (19), and (20), one deduces that horizonless curved spacetimes of spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations are generally characterized by an even (possibly zero) number of closed light rings [7, 8, 28].
L67: The (in)stability of null circular geodesics in the curved spacetime (3) is determined by the second spatial derivative of the radially-dependent curvature potential (16) [18, 25]. In particular, stable light rings which, as discussed in [11–13], are associated with non-linear dynamical instabilities of massless fields in the corresponding curved spacetimes, are characterized by locally convex curvature potentials with the radial property V_r''(r = r_\gamma) &gt; 0 [18, 25].
L68: From Eqs. (4), (5), (16), (17), and the conservation equation T_{r;\mu}^\mu = 0 [22], one obtains the radial functional relation [8, 9]
L69: V_r''(r = r_\gamma) = -\frac{E^2 e^{2\delta}}{\mu r_\gamma} \times \mathcal{R}'(r = r_\gamma) \quad (21)
L70: for the second radial derivative of the effective curvature potential (16). Interestingly, taking cognizance of Eqs. (17) and (19) one finds that, in general [28], the innermost null circular geodesic of a spatially regular self-gravitating matter configuration is characterized by the properties
L71: \mathcal{R}(r = r_\gamma^{\text{innermost}}) = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{R}'(r = r_\gamma^{\text{innermost}}) &lt; 0. \quad (22)
L72: From Eqs. (21) and (22) one deduces that the innermost null circular geodesic of a spatially regular self-gravitating matter configuration is stable. In particular, it is characterized by the property [7–9]
L73: V_r''(r = r_\gamma^{\text{innermost}}) &gt; 0. \quad (23)
L74: As discussed above, the presence of the stable light ring (22) in the curved spacetime (3) implies that the spatially regular self-gravitating matter configuration is non-linearly unstable to massless perturbation fields [11, 12, 14]. One therefore concludes that physically realistic (that is, stable) self-gravitating matter configurations have no null circular geodesics (no closed light rings) with the functional properties (22). In particular, taking cognizance of Eqs. (17), (19), and (20), one deduces that spatially regular stable matter configurations are characterized by the radial property
L75: R(r) &gt; 0 \quad \text{for} \quad r \in [0, \infty], \quad (24)
L76: which yields the upper bound [see Eq. (18)]
L77: \mathcal{F}(r) &lt; \frac{1}{2}[3\mu(r) - 1] \quad \text{for} \quad r \in [0, \infty] \quad (25)
L78: on the dimensionless force function (2).
L79: IV. SUMMARY
L80: In the physically important papers [1, 2] Gibbons and Schiller have conjectured that the compact mathematical relation \mathcal{F} \leq c^4/4G provides an upper bound on the magnitude of forces in general relativity. Motivated by this intriguing conjecture, in the present paper we have raised the physically interesting question: Can a maximum force relation of the form \mathcal{F} \leq \eta \cdot c^4/G with \eta = O(1) be derived for spatially regular self-gravitating stable matter configurations in curved spacetimes?
L81: In order to derive the desired upper bound on the dimensionless force function \mathcal{F} \equiv 4\pi r^2 p(r), we have pointed out that the above stated question is closely related to the recent theorem presented in [7] (see also [8, 9]) which, when combined with the physically interesting results presented in [11, 12], asserts that horizonless curved spacetimes of spatially regular self-gravitating matter configurations that possess closed light rings (null circular geodesics) are dynamically unstable to non-linear massless fields.
L82: Using this characteristic instability property of self-gravitating matter configurations that possess null circular geodesics, we have explicitly proved that the answer to the above stated intriguing question is ‘Yes!’. In particular, using the non-linearly coupled Einstein-matter field equations, we have proved that the force function \mathcal{F} \equiv 4\pi r^2 p(r) in curved spacetimes of stable self-gravitating matter configurations is bounded from above by the remarkably compact relation [see Eqs. (12) and (25)]
L83: \mathcal{F} &lt; \frac{c^4}{G}. \quad (26)
